./data/input_representation_atlas_crop_crossvalidation_2026-07-12/post_role_crossvalidated_crop_ledger.json:9233:        "depicted_input": "Figure 1’s overview jointly shows the protein input object as an amino-acid sequence in the Proteins modality panel and the linked protein foundation model context via the “Proteins: ESM3” backbone entry, making the protein-to-ESM3 input mechanism visible at overview level.",
./data/input_representation_atlas_crop_crossvalidation_2026-07-12/post_role_crossvalidated_crop_ledger.json:9234:        "rationale": "The current crop is too narrow because it isolates only the Proteins task panel and drops the adjacent backbone context. A better crop exists in the same figure: include the full left-to-center overview through the Biological Modalities section so the reader can see both the protein sequence representation and the paper’s protein foundation model reference (ESM3). Under the atlas rules, a shared architecture/overview figure can support a route when it visibly depicts the route-specific input object or representation, and a model box is not mandatory. This is defensible for the ESM-3 protein embedding route at a high level, but not for the protein metadata prompt, because the figure does not show the structured text prompt blocks or prompt-token interface.",
./data/input_representation_atlas_crop_crossvalidation_2026-07-12/post_role_crossvalidated_crop_ledger.json:9387:      "caption": "Figure 1: Landscape of TeamPath (a) Steps of dataset curation. We extract image-text pairs from a processed TCGA dataset (PathGen-1.6M). (b) Word cloud visualization of ROI captions (upper) and questions (bottom). (c) The core visual language model architecture of TeamPath. (d) TeamPath as a system with an LLM-enhanced router (with over 80% accuracy in choosing the correct approach) and the corresponding capacities in various downstream applications. The logo fire means that we need to adjust the parameters of models, and the logo snowflake means that we do not change the parameters. (e) Overall ranking list of different methods across tasks and metrics. A lower rank (larger bubble) means a better method.",
./data/input_representation_atlas_crop_crossvalidation_2026-07-12/post_role_crossvalidated_crop_ledger.json:9688:      "caption": "Figure 1: An Overview of scDiff .",
./data/input_representation_atlas_crop_crossvalidation_2026-07-12/post_role_crossvalidated_crop_ledger.json:9699:    "panel_label": "Figure 1",
./data/input_representation_atlas_crop_crossvalidation_2026-07-12/post_role_crossvalidated_crop_ledger.json:9924:      "caption": "Figure 1: Overview of SciCore-Omics. a , Model architecture of SciCore-Omics. Histological images and spatial gene expression profiles are encoded by modality-specific encoders, and projected into the language-model space. b , Unified tri-modal token representation. Image, gene virtual tokens and text tokens are jointly modelled in a shared decoder-based backbone. c , Representative downstream tasks supported by SciCore-Omics across multiple biological scales.",
./data/input_representation_atlas_crop_crossvalidation_2026-07-12/post_role_crossvalidated_crop_ledger.json:10409:      "caption": "Figure 1: Landscape of TeamPath (a) Steps of dataset curation. We extract image-text pairs from a processed TCGA dataset (PathGen-1.6M). (b) Word cloud visualization of ROI captions (upper) and questions (bottom). (c) The core visual language model architecture of TeamPath. (d) TeamPath as a system with an LLM-enhanced router (with over 80% accuracy in choosing the correct approach) and the corresponding capacities in various downstream applications. The logo fire means that we need to adjust the parameters of models, and the logo snowflake means that we do not change the parameters. (e) Overall ranking list of different methods across tasks and metrics. A lower rank (larger bubble) means a better method.",
./data/input_representation_atlas_crop_crossvalidation_2026-07-12/post_role_crossvalidated_crop_ledger.json:10643:      "caption": "Figure 1: Overview of the TISSUENARRATOR framework. A. TISSUENARRATOR takes as input a tissue patch, encodes individual cells into cell sentences, and assembles a spatial sentence from neighboring cells. The spatial sentence can be interpreted by an LLM with biological prior knowledge and contextual understanding to predict gene expressions in a cell and to answer questions about tissues. Text output of the LLM can be further transformed into gene expression levels. B. TISSUENARRATOR supports four downstream tasks: cell generation, neighborhood perturbation, genetic perturbation, and conversational tissue querying. For cell generation tasks, model-generated cell sentences can be converted back into gene expression space.",
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000248.md:52:Figure 1: Overview of the Cell2Text framework. The model takes single-cell RNA-seq profiles as input and processes them through a pretrained Geneformer encoder to generate contextualized gene-level embeddings. These embeddings are projected into the semantic space of the language model via a lightweight adapter module, aligning biological signals with linguistic representations. A pretrained, instruction-tuned LLM decoder then generates structured natural language descriptions that capture cellular identity, tissue of origin, disease associations, and pathway activity.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000248.md:343:The cumulative distribution function (CDF) in Figure 10 further illustrates this property, with the sharp rise in the curve at low similarity values confirming that a large fraction of pairwise similarities are small. This heavy-tailed nature of the distribution is crucial, as it demonstrates the metric's ability to effectively discriminate between the few closely related cell types and the many unrelated ones, which is essential for our nuanced evaluation of model predictions.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000248.md:361:Figure 10: Cumulative distribution of similarity scores.
