ncing large language models for biological pathway reasoning, 2025a. URL https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.16660 .\n- Haiteng Zhao, Chang Ma, Fangzhi Xu, Lingpeng Kong, and Zhi-Hong Deng. BioMaze dataset. https://huggingface.co/datasets/haitengzhao/BioMaze , 2025b. Apache-2.0 license.\n\nA Appendix\nA.1 Instruction-Tuning Training Task Inventory\nTable 6 summarizes the training tasks at the task-family level. We group closely related tasks to keep the inventory readable; concrete task names are the dataset identifiers used by the data preparation and training pipeline. The scale column reports generated training examples after filtering and subsampling.\nTable 6: Instruction-tuning training task inventory. Representative prompt templates are lightly normalized from the data-preparation configs; each task family uses multiple paraphrases during generation.\n\nHCA, Training examples = 2.17M. HCA, Task families = cell type, tissue, cell-cycle phase, lineage, biological role. HCA, Output = cell type, tissue, phase, lineage, role. HCA, Example template = 'Given this expression profile, identify the cell type / tissue / cell-cycle phase.'. TCGA, Training examples = 97.9k. TCGA, Task families = study, tissue, tumor/normal status, stage, demographics, age. TCGA, Output = clinical label, numeric age. TCGA, Example template = 'Based on this expression profile, determine the TCGA study, primary site, stage, or patient attribute.'. GTEx, Training examples = 14.8k. GTEx, Task families = tissue of origin. GTEx, Output = tissue label. GTEx, Example template = 'Classify the tissue of origin from the expression profile.'. GEO, Training examples = 1.63M. GEO, Task families = sample descriptions, top gene prediction, metadata annotation, derived conversations and QA. GEO, Output = sample description, class, gene list, yes/no, natural-language response. GEO, Example template = 'Describe this sample from its expression profile.' / 'Compare the disease and control samples.'. DepMap, Training examples = 287k. DepMap, Task families = gene essentiality, including binned evaluation by expression context. DepMap, Output = yes/no essentiality. DepMap, Example template = 'Given this cell-line expression profile, is GENE essential for survival? Answer yes or no.'. Replogle / Nadig, Training examples = 123k. Replogle / Nadig, Task families = top expressed genes, knockout identification, perturbation response prediction. Replogle / Nadig, Output = knocked-out gene, ranked gene list. Replogle / Nadig, Example template = 'Given the control profile, predict the top expressed genes after knocking out GENE .'. BioMaze / STRING / Gene Ontology, Training examples = 1.04M. BioMaze / STRING / Gene Ontology, Task families = graph reachability, shortest paths, pathway completion, gene-set enrichment. BioMaze / STRING / Gene Ontology, Output = yes/no, protein path, pathway name, gene list. BioMaze / STRING / Gene Ontology, Example template = 'With these genes knocked out, what is the shortest downstream path from A to B ?'. DepMap / Cellosaurus / UniProt, Training examples = 212k. DepMap / Cellosaurus / UniProt, Task families = mutations, copy number, disease, lineage, anatomical site, transformant status, cell-line description/similarity, protein function/location. DepMap / Cellosaurus / UniProt, Output = entity list, cell-line name, short answer. DepMap / Cellosaurus / UniProt, Example template = 'Which cancer genes are mutated in this cell line?' / 'What are possible locations of this protein?'\n\nA Appendix\nA.1 Instruction-Tuning Training Task Inventory\nDetailed descriptions of the underlying data sources are provided in Appendix A.2.\n\nA Appendix\nA.2 Datasets\nAll expression datasets used in this study were aligned to a unified set of 19,260 protein-coding and mitochondrial Ensembl genes derived from GENCODE annotations [Mudge et al., 2025]. For single-cell datasets, we retained raw count data prior to normalization, while for bulk expression datasets, we used precomputed transcripts per million (TPM) values.\n\nA Appendix\nA.2.1 Human Cell Atlas\nThe Human Cell Atlas (HCA) [Regev et al., 2017] was accessed via the CellxGene Census (release 2025-01-30) [Chan Zuckerberg Initiative, 2025], from which we downloaded 27 datasets comprising 6 , 161 , 559 cells spanning diverse tissues, developmental stages, and donors. HCA forms the primary source of training examples for single-cell tasks.\n\nA Appendix\nA.2.2 Tabula Sapiens\nTabula Sapiens [The Tabula Sapiens Consortium, 2022] is a multi-organ atlas covering 24 tissues from multiple donors. It is used exclusively as a held-out evaluation benchmark and is excluded from training. We use the GEO-hosted version of Tabula Sapiens (GSE201048; 483,152 cells), which is the version reported by CellWhisperer [Schaefer et al., 2025], enabling direct comparison. The dataset provides standardized cell-level metadata including cell type, tissue of origin, developmental lineage, cell-cycle phase, and donor sex.\n\n\nA Appendix A.2.2 Tabula Sapiens Table 7: Summary of expression datasets used for training and evaluation. All datasets are aligned to the same set of 19,260 protein-coding genes. N pert denotes the number of distinct genetic perturbations; - indicates an observational study. Observational, Samples = . Observational, Genes = . Observational, N pert = . Observational, Modality = . Observational, Study type = . Human Cell Atlas [Regev et al., 2017], Samples = 6,161,559. Human Cell Atlas [Regev et al., 2017], Genes = 19,260. Human Cell Atlas [Regev et al., 2017], N pert = -. Human Cell Atlas [Regev et al., 2017], Modality = scRNA-seq. Human Cell Atlas [Regev et al., 2017], Study type = Observational. Tabula Sapiens (GEO) [The Tabula Sapiens Consortium, 2022], Samples = 483,152. Tabula Sapiens (GEO) [The Tabula Sapiens Consortium, 2022], Genes = 19,260. Tabula Sapiens (GEO) [The Tabula Sapiens Consortium, 2022], N pert = -. Tabula Sapiens (GEO) [The Tabula Sapiens Consortium, 2022], Modality = scRNA-seq. Tabula Sapiens (GEO) [The Tabula Sapiens Consortium, 2022], Study type = Observational. GEO [Clough et al., 2024], Samples = 615,595. GEO [Clough et al., 2024], Genes = 19,260. GEO [Clough et al., 2024], N pert = -. GEO [Clough et al., 2024], Modality = Bulk RNA-seq. GEO [Clough et al., 2024], Study type = Observational. GTEx v10 [GTEx Consortium, 2020], Samples = 19,616. GTEx v10 [GTEx Consortium, 2020], Genes = 19,260. GTEx v10 [GTEx Consortium, 2020], N pert = -. GTEx v10 [GTEx Consortium, 2020], Modality = Bulk RNA-seq. GTEx v10 [GTEx Consortium, 2020], Study type = Observational. TCGA [Weinstein et al., 2013], Samples = 11,504. TCGA [Weinstein et al., 2013], Genes = 19,260. TCGA [Weinstein et al., 2013], N pert = -. TCGA [Weinstein et al., 2013], Modality = Bulk RNA-seq. TCGA [Weinstein et al., 2013], Study type = Observational. Human Diseases [Schaefer et al., 2025], Samples = 14,112. Human Diseases [Schaefer et al., 2025], Genes = 19,260. Human Diseases [Schaefer et al., 2025], N pert = -. Human Diseases [Schaefer et al., 2025], Modality = Bulk RNA-seq. Human Diseases [Schaefer et al., 2025], Study type = Observational. DepMap Expression 24Q4 [DepMap, 2024], Samples = 1,673. DepMap Expression 24Q4 [DepMap, 2024], Genes = 19,260.\n\nDepMap Expression 24Q4 [DepMap, 2024], N\n\nA Appendix\nA.2.2 Tabula Sapiens\n pert = -. DepMap Expression 24Q4 [DepMap, 2024], Modality = Bulk RNA-seq. DepMap Expression 24Q4 [DepMap, 2024], Study type = Observational. Perturbational, Samples = . Perturbational, Genes = . Perturbational, N pert = . Perturbational, Modality = . Perturbational, Study type = . DepMap Gene Dependency 24Q4 [DepMap, 2024], Samples = 1,178. DepMap Gene Dependency 24Q4 [DepMap, 2024], Genes = -. DepMap Gene Dependency 24Q4 [DepMap, 2024], N pert = 17,916. DepMap Gene Dependency 24Q4 [DepMap, 2024], Modality = CRISPR. DepMap Gene Dependency 24Q4 [DepMap, 2024], Study type = Genetic KO. Replogle et al. 2022 [Replogle et al., 2022], Samples = 2,546,823. Replogle et al. 2022 [Replogle et al., 2022], Genes = 19,260. Replogle et al. 2022 [Replogle et al., 2022], N pert = 9,859. Replogle et al. 2022 [Replogle et al., 2022], Modality = scRNA-seq. Replogle et al. 2022 [Replogle et al., 2022], Study type = Genetic KD. Nadig et al. 2025 [Nadig et al., 2025], Samples = 408,230. Nadig et al. 2025 [Nadig et al., 2025], Genes = 19,260. Nadig et al. 2025 [Nadig et al., 2025], N pert = 2,390. Nadig et al. 2025 [Nadig et al., 2025], Modality = scRNA-seq. Nadig et al. 2025 [Nadig et al., 2025], Study type = Genetic KD\n\nA Appendix\nA.2.2 Tabula Sapiens\nA.2.3 Gene Expression Omnibus (GEO)\nThe ARCHS4 [Lachmann et al., 2018] database is a uniformly processed compendium of human and mouse bulk RNA-seq data aggregated from the Gene Expression Omnibus (GEO) [Clough et al., 2024]. To obtain expression profiles for selected GEO studies, we used the ARCHS4 opensource processing pipeline, available under the Apache License 2.0, to uniformly re-process raw RNA-seq data from GEO. We filtered samples by single-cell probability, library selection, and library source, yielding 615 , 595 human bulk RNA-seq samples with TPM transcript-level expression values. Associated sample metadata, downloaded directly from GEO, is unstructured and highly heterogeneous, posing a significant challenge for standardization at training scale. We addressed this with an LLM-based curation pipeline that (i) generated free-text natural language descriptions for each sample, and (ii) extracted structured annotations including tissue, cell type, cell line, disease, and perturbation target. Both the generated descriptions and structured annotations were used as supervision signals during instruction tuning.\nFor all GEO-derived tasks, including the GEO-OmicsQA benchmark, we split data at the publication / study level rather than at the sample level. Samples, metadata-derived prompts, and literature-derived QA examples from the same publication are assigned to a single split, preventing validation or test examples from sharing source publications with training examples.\n\nA Appendix\nA.2.2 Tabula Sapiens\nA.2.4 Human Diseases\nThe Human Diseases dataset, assembled by Schaefer et al. [2025], is a curated collection of 14 , 112 bulk RNA-seq samples across 27 tissue types covering a broad spectrum of disease contexts. Each sample carries a tissue-of-origin label and one of 229 free-text disease subtype labels. The 5 , 683 samples overlapping with our GEO training corpus were removed from GEO prior to training to prevent data leakage.\n\nA Appendix\nA.2.2 Tabula Sapiens\nA.2.5 Genotype-Tissue Expression (GTEx)\nThe Genotype-Tissue Expression (GTEx) project [GTEx Consortium, 2020] provides bulk RNA-seq profiles from postmortem donors across 54 tissue sites, designed to characterize tissue-specific patterns of gene expression and regulation. We use the v10 release, comprising 19,616 TPMnormalized samples spanning the full range of profiled tissues, which we use for tissue-of-origin prediction.\n\nA Appendix\nA.2.2 Tabula Sapiens\nA.2.6 The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA)\nThe Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) [Weinstein et al., 2013] is a landmark cancer genomics program that collected tumor and matched normal tissue samples from over 11,000 patients across 33 cancer types. We downloaded TPM-normalized expression data from the GDC Open Access portal, along with clinical metadata from the TCGA Pan-Cancer Clinical Data Resource [Liu et al., 2018], which provides standardized clinical outcome endpoints (overall survival, disease-specific survival, diseasefree interval, and progression-free interval) and key demographic and pathological annotations across the full pan-cancer cohort.\n\nA Appendix\nA.2.7 DepMap\nThe Cancer Dependency Map [DepMap, 2024, Pacini et al., 2024] is a systematic resource profiling cancer cell lines across multiple molecular modalities, including bulk RNA-seq expression, genomewide CRISPR knockout screens, DNA mutations, copy number alterations, and proteomics. For each cell line, CRISPR-derived Chronos gene-effect and gene dependency scores quantify the fitness consequence of individual gene knockouts, enabling binary essentiality labels at the (cell line, gene) level. We use the 24Q4 release, spanning diverse tissue lineages and cancer types.\n\nA Appendix\nA.2.7 DepMap\nA.2.8 Replogle and Nadig Datasets\nReplogle et al. [Replogle et al., 2022] and Nadig et al. [Nadig et al., 2025] are large-scale Perturbseq experiments combining CRISPR knockdown with single-cell transcriptomics to measure the genome-wide transcriptional response to thousands of individual gene perturbations. Replogle et al. profiled K562 (chronic myeloid leukemia) and RPE1 (retinal pigment epithelium) cell lines across 9,859 perturbations; Nadig et al. profiled HEPG2 (hepatocellular carcinoma) and JURKAT (T-cell leukemia) cell lines across 2,390 perturbations. Each dataset includes unperturbed control cells serving as a reference baseline for perturbation prediction tasks.\n\nA Appendix\nA.2.7 DepMap\nA.2.9 Biological Networks\nUnlike the expression datasets above, pathway graphs, protein-protein interaction (PPI) networks, and gene set collections do not provide transcriptomic profiles; they are used solely to construct natural-language graph-reasoning tasks over biological interaction networks and gene program annotations.\nWe use the curated biological pathway graph database provided by Zhao et al. [Zhao et al., 2025a], derived from KEGG [Kanehisa et al., 2025] (Kyoto Encyclopedia of Genes and Genomes). This graph encodes directed regulatory and metabolic relationships between proteins and small molecules, representing expert-curated knowledge of cellular processes across multiple organisms.\nSTRING [Szklarczyk et al., 2023] is a database of known and predicted protein-protein interactions, integrating evidence from experimental data, co-expression, text mining, and computational prediction. We used STRING v12.0 restricted to human interactions (taxonomy ID 9606). Combined interaction scores were recalculated following the STRING protocol: each per-channel score was prior-corrected as s ∗ = ( s -p ) / (1 -p ) with prior p = 0 . 041 , and database and text-mining channels were excluded from the recombined score to avoid circularity with curated annotations. Four ubiquitous hub proteins (UBB, UBC, RPS27A, UBA52) were removed prior to filtering. We retained edges with a corrected combined score ≥ 700 , or ≥ 150 when additionally supported by a database score ≥ 400 , yielding a high-confidence interaction set.\nThe Gene Ontology (GO) [Ashburner et al., 2000, Gene Ontology Consortium, 2025a] is a collaborative, continuously updated resource providing structured controlled vocabularies describing gene product functions across species. We downloaded Biological Process annotations from the GO knowledgebase (release 2025-06-01 [Gene Ontology Consortium, 2025b]) and used them to associate genes with high-level functional programs, enabling natural-language tasks grounded in curated gene program membership.\n\nA Appendix\nA.2.10 Cellosaurus\nCellosaurus [Bairoch, 2018] is a comprehensive knowledge resource for cell lines, providing standardized nomenclature and curated biological annotations. For each cancer cell line in our DepMap catalogue, we retrieved annotations from the Cellosaurus REST API using the corresponding accession identifier ( CVCL_* ), including cell category, disease name with ontology identifier, transformant agent, and associated literature references. These annotations were merged with existing cell-line metadata to enrich natural-language tasks requiring the model to map an expression profile or cell-line name to its biological provenance and disease context.\n\nA Appendix\nA.2.11 UniProt\nUniProt [The UniProt Consortium, 2025] is a comprehensive protein sequence and functional annotation database. We downloaded the manually reviewed Swiss-Prot flat file from the official UniProt FTP server and filtered it to human proteins only. For each protein, we extracted identity fields, functional descriptions, controlled-vocabulary keywords, subcellular localization, and protein class annotations. These were used to construct natural-language tasks requiring the model to answer questions about protein properties given a gene name.\n\nA Appendix\nA.3 Benchmarks\nA.3.1 Constrained Likelihood Scoring\nFor closed-set classification tasks, each candidate label is treated as a possible assistant continuation. We encode the prompt once, score each label token sequence under teacher forcing, sum token log probabilities, and normalize by label length. The top-scoring label gives accuracy. To compute macro AUROC, we apply a softmax over the candidate label scores for each example, compute one-vs-rest AUROC for each class, and average over classes. We report these likelihood-based AUROC values where prior work uses classifier-style metrics, making comparisons less sensitive to answer formatting and more comparable to embedding-based or specialist models.\n\nA Appendix\nA.3 Benchmarks\nA.3.2 DeepEval Judge for Open-Ended Generation\nOpen-ended generation tasks, including GEO-OmicsQA simple and contextual tasks, are scored with DeepEval GEval [Liu et al., 2023b] using the Gemini judge model gemini-3.1-flash-lite-preview [Google DeepMind, 2026a]. Each judged test case is passed to GEval with three fields: the input question, the model's actual output, and the expected reference output. The reported judge score is the mean DeepEval score.\nThe judge configuration is adapted from the evaluation code as follows:\n```\nDEEPEVAL_EVALUATION_STEPS = [ \"Read the input question to understand what biological \" \"information is being asked for.\", \"Identify every key biological entity in the expected output: \" \"gene names, cell types or cell lines, tissues, organisms, \" \"diseases, treatments, compounds, and signaling pathways.\", \"Check whether the actual output mentions the same key entities \" \"or their accepted synonyms and aliases. Additional correct \" \"details beyond the expected output should not be penalized.\", \"For directional claims in the expected output, verify that the \" \"actual output states the same direction.\", \"Evaluate whether the biological interpretation and mechanistic \" \"reasoning are consistent with the expected output.\", \"Assess completeness: missing the central finding or conclusion \" \"is a significant deficiency.\", ] DEEPEVAL_RUBRIC = [ Rubric(score_range=(0, 2), expected_outcome=\"Irrelevant, unanswered, or wrong subject.\"), Rubric(score_range=(3, 4), expected_outcome=\"Correct topic but major factual errors.\"), Rubric(score_range=(5, 6), expected_outcome=\"Partially correct but incomplete or inaccurate.\"), Rubric(score_range=(7, 8), expected_outcome=\"Main finding and most entities correct.\"), Rubric(score_range=(9, 10), expected_outcome=\"All key entities, directions, and conclusions align.\"), ] geval = GEval(\n```\n```\nname=\"DeepEval\", evaluation_steps=DEEPEVAL_EVALUATION_STEPS, rubric=DEEPEVAL_RUBRIC, evaluation_params=[ LLMTestCaseParams.INPUT, LLMTestCaseParams.ACTUAL_OUTPUT, LLMTestCaseParams.EXPECTED_OUTPUT, ], model=GeminiModel(model=\"gemini-3.1-flash-lite-preview\"), )\n```\n\nA Appendix\nA.3 Benchmarks\nA.3.3 Perturbation Prediction\nPerturbation prediction is a core capability of Cell2Sentence-Scale [Levine et al., 2024]. However, the perturbation datasets used in their paper are evaluated on a restricted gene subset inherited from prior chemical perturbation benchmarks [Hetzel et al., 2022], which includes a substantial proportion of non-protein-coding features such as pseudogenes and non-coding RNAs alongside the landmark and highly variable genes. This creates an asymmetry with models operating over full protein-coding transcriptomic profiles and limits the biological scope of the evaluation. To compare OmicsLM against Cell2Sentence-Scale on this task over a consistent, biologically interpretable gene space, we constructed a dedicated held-out benchmark from the X-Atlas/Orion dataset [Huang et al., 2025], a genome-wide CRISPRi screen targeting all human protein-coding genes across two cell lines: HCT116 and HEK293T. Neither model was exposed to this dataset during training, making it a genuine out-of-distribution evaluation for both. For benchmarking, we focus exclusively on the HCT116 colorectal cancer cell line.\nExpression profiles were restricted to 19 , 260 protein-coding genes by intersecting the full 38 , 606 -gene X-Atlas/Orion reference with a curated human proteome reference. Pseudobulk profiles were computed by averaging raw counts across all cells sharing the same perturbation target; perturbations supported by fewer than 10 cells were excluded.\nTo stratify perturbations by transcriptional effect size, we ran PDEX [Arc Institute, 2025], a parallelized Wilcoxon rank-sum test with tie correction, comparing each perturbation against 1 , 500 randomly subsampled non-targeting control cells. Fold-change values of exactly 20, arising from undefined (zero-denominator) comparisons in PDEX, were treated as missing. Per-perturbation BenjaminiHochberg FDR correction was applied across all genes; the number of significant DEGs (FDR ≤ 0 . 05 , | log 2 FC | ≥ 0 . 5 ) was used solely as a stratification criterion. Perturbations were assigned to four tiers: negligible ( 0 -10 DEGs), subtle ( 11 -50 ), medium ( 51 -500 ), and strong ( > 500 ). The negligible tier was excluded as transcriptional responses at this level are indistinguishable from noise. We randomly sampled 70 perturbations per tier from the remaining three strata (fixed random seed for reproducibility), yielding a balanced benchmark of 210 perturbations spanning the full range of detectable transcriptional responses.\nBoth models are evaluated on the task of predicting the post-perturbation cell sentence: given the unperturbed control expression profile as input, each model is asked to generate the ranked list of most highly expressed protein-coding genes in the perturbed cell. The ground-truth cell sentence for each perturbation is constructed from the pseudobulk expression profile by ranking all non-zero expressed protein-coding genes by mean expression in descending order. Model predictions are evaluated using Recall@50, measuring how many of the top-50 predicted genes appear among the true top-50 most highly expressed genes after perturbation.\n\nA Appendix\nA.4 Training Details\nWe train OmicsLM with supervised causal language modeling on the conversational records described in Section 4, using full fine-tuning rather than parameter-efficient adapters: the Qwen3 language backbone, token embeddings, added gene-token embeddings, and omics projection layer are updated jointly. Following standard supervised instruction tuning [Ouyang et al., 2022], the loss is applied only to assistant response tokens; user instructions, system text, and multimodal placeholder tokens are included in the context but masked from the training objective.\nOmicsLM is trained on a single HGX H100 node with 8 NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3 GPUs for two epochs with maximum context length 1024, bf16 precision, FlashAttention-2 [Dao, 2024], DeepSpeed ZeRO-2 [Rajbhandari et al., 2020], and supervised fine-tuning. The selected checkpoint took approximately 22 hours of training wall-clock time, excluding downstream inference and evaluation. Optimization uses per-device batch size 16, gradient accumulation of 2, global batch size 256, cosine learning-rate decay, 10% warmup, learning rate 1 . 5 × 10 -5 , and NEFTune noise α = 5 [Jain et al., 2024].\n\nA Appendix\nA.5 Ablation Studies\nTable 8: Ablation study results. Tasks use the same metrics as in Section 6; higher is better for task metrics and for average relative rank. Columns are grouped by benchmark family. Rows with repeated runs report mean ± standard deviation over three runs; all other rows are single runs. The average relative rank column is computed per block: for each task we rank the rows in that block (fractional ranks for ties), normalize to [0 , 1] where 1 is best and 0 is worst, and average across tasks. Cell2Sentence-like is a text-only control trained with the top 100 most expressed genes in place of transcriptomic embeddings.\n\nA Appendix A.5 Ablation Studies Backbone size, Avg. rel. rank = . Backbone size, Human Diseases.Disease subtype = . Backbone size, Human Diseases.Tissue = . Backbone size, Tissue = . Backbone size, Tabula.Cell type = . Backbone size, Sapiens.Cell type top-20 = . Backbone size, GTEx.Purity = . Backbone size, GTEx.Tissue = . Backbone size, X-Atlas/Orion.HCT116 = . Backbone size, GEO-OmicsQA.Binary QA = . Backbone size, GEO-OmicsQA.Free text = . Qwen3-8B, Avg. rel. rank = .575. Qwen3-8B, Human Diseases.Disease subtype = .863 ± .008. Qwen3-8B, Human Diseases.Tissue = .961 ± .008. Qwen3-8B, Tissue = .714 ± .013. Qwen3-8B, Tabula.Cell type = .875 ± .015. Qwen3-8B, Sapiens.Cell type top-20 = .939 ± .012. Qwen3-8B, GTEx.Purity = .926 ± .002. Qwen3-8B, GTEx.Tissue = .986 ± .004. Qwen3-8B, X-Atlas/Orion.HCT116 = .649 ± .015. Qwen3-8B, GEO-OmicsQA.Binary QA = .759 ± .002. Qwen3-8B, GEO-OmicsQA.Free text = .622 ± .003. Qwen3-1.7B, Avg. rel. rank = .500. Qwen3-1.7B, Human Diseases.Disease subtype = .833 ± .006. Qwen3-1.7B, Human Diseases.Tissue = .976 ± .013. Qwen3-1.7B, Tissue = .730 ± .014. Qwen3-1.7B, Tabula.Cell type = .901 ± .004. Qwen3-1.7B, Sapiens.Cell type top-20 = .950 ± .003. Qwen3-1.7B, GTEx.Purity = .919 ± .007. Qwen3-1.7B, GTEx.Tissue = .986 ± .004. Qwen3-1.7B, X-Atlas/Orion.HCT116 = .573 ± .009. Qwen3-1.7B, GEO-OmicsQA.Binary QA = .738 ± .001. Qwen3-1.7B, GEO-OmicsQA.Free text = .590 ± .005. Qwen3-0.6B, Avg. rel. rank = .425. Qwen3-0.6B, Human Diseases.Disease subtype = .803. Qwen3-0.6B, Human Diseases.Tissue = .976. Qwen3-0.6B, Tissue = .745. Qwen3-0.6B, Tabula.Cell type = .882. Qwen3-0.6B, Sapiens