./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003434_0c79637be8b9/full_2026_07_06_rec_003434_0c79637be8b9_docling_json_20260812_201130/docling_graph/graph.json:363:      "supporting_figure_or_table": "Figure 1",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003434_0c79637be8b9/full_2026_07_06_rec_003434_0c79637be8b9_docling_json_20260812_201130/debug/trace_data.json:57:            "text_content": "The most pronounced advantage was observed in RNA-based cancer prognosis, where the SFT model achieved 0.77 accuracy on TCGA pairwise progression-free survival comparisons (which patient lived longer without progression?). In NHANES binary 10-year mortality prediction, the SFT model placed first with 0.89 accuracy, ahead of Gemini 3 Pro which was the best model previously tested in Longevity Bench. The regression variant of the NHANES time-to-death task yielded the lowest MAE among all tested systems (51.0 months). Our SFT model also achieved 0.60 accuracy on GTEx pairwise age comparisons (which sample is older?), a task category in which most previously tested LLMs struggled to exceed random baseline.\n\nThese results indicate that structured fine-tuning on aging-related tasks can close the performance gap between smaller models and frontier systems of substantially greater capacity. The  improvements  are  seen  in  three  distinct  biodata  modalities,  suggesting  that  the  acquired knowledge generalizes across data types rather than being confined to a single domain.\n\nFigure 1. Longevity-LLM shows competitive performance in a range of Longevity Bench tasks. The tested tasks span the domains of transcriptomics, proteomics, and clinical record. Base Qwen-3 14B failed to produce valid outputs on all tasks.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific performance figure composed of bar charts comparing model accuracy or error across biomedical/health prediction tasks.\n\nVisible panels:\n- TCGA Survival (Binary): accuracy bar chart with chance baseline around 0.50.\n- NHANES Mortality (Binary): accuracy bar chart with chance baseline around 0.50.\n- NHANES Mortality (Pairwise): accuracy bar chart with chance baseline around 0.50.\n- NHANES Mortality (Multiclass): accuracy bar chart with chance baseline around 0.25.\n- GTEx Aging (Pairwise): accuracy bar chart with chance baseline around 0.50.\n- Proteomic Aging (Pairwise): accuracy bar chart wit... [truncated, total 4404 chars]",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003434_0c79637be8b9/full_2026_07_06_rec_003434_0c79637be8b9_docling_json_20260812_201130/debug/trace_data.json:333:                  "supporting_figure_or_table": "Figure 1",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_01/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001519_57bf8abbd7af/full_2026_07_06_rec_001519_57bf8abbd7af_docling_json_20260812_195601/docling/document.json:2488:      "orig": "Figure 1 illustrates the high-level architecture of BioGPT, showcasing its end-to-end pipeline from input tokenization to rare disease diagnosis. The framework ingests genomic sequences (tokenized as k-mers) and clinical narratives (tokenized via BPE), which are independently embedded and passed through a multi-modal transformer fusion module. This fusion module integrates encoded signals from both modalities, enabling a holistic representation of patient data. The generative decoder  then  outputs  natural  language  explanations  alongside  structured  predictions.  The  architecture  supports  both pretraining and fine-tuning, and incorporates attention visualization, offering transparent, interpretable diagnostics.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_01/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001519_57bf8abbd7af/full_2026_07_06_rec_001519_57bf8abbd7af_docling_json_20260812_195601/docling/document.json:2489:      "text": "Figure 1 illustrates the high-level architecture of BioGPT, showcasing its end-to-end pipeline from input tokenization to rare disease diagnosis. The framework ingests genomic sequences (tokenized as k-mers) and clinical narratives (tokenized via BPE), which are independently embedded and passed through a multi-modal transformer fusion module. This fusion module integrates encoded signals from both modalities, enabling a holistic representation of patient data. The generative decoder  then  outputs  natural  language  explanations  alongside  structured  predictions.  The  architecture  supports  both pretraining and fine-tuning, and incorporates attention visualization, offering transparent, interpretable diagnostics."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003434_0c79637be8b9/full_2026_07_06_rec_003434_0c79637be8b9_docling_json_20260812_201130/docling_graph/nodes.csv:7:"TaxonomyCodedRoute_f28f988d17878473","TaxonomyCodedRoute","entity","TaxonomyCodedRoute","Performance in Longevity Bench","Qwen3-14B SFT","evaluation","evaluated it against 15 frontier LLMs across seven Longevity Bench tasks spanning tasks in the domains of oncology prognosis, age and mortality prediction","seven Longevity Bench tasks spanning tasks in the domains of oncology prognosis, age and mortality prediction","Longevity Bench biomedical benchmark tasks","text-native biomedical benchmark prompts","['evaluated it against 15 frontier LLMs', 'seven Longevity Bench tasks', 'ranked first in four out of seven tasks']","['benchmark prompts -> model predictions -> performance comparison against frontier LLMs']","our SFT model","text_native_token_stream","structured_biological_prompt_or_task_scaffold","evaluated it against 15 frontier LLMs across seven Longevity Bench tasks","tokenizer_sequence","instruction_or_query","actual_model_input","evaluated it against 15 frontier LLMs across seven Longevity Bench tasks","Performance in Longevity Bench","Figure 1","explicit_text","","{""document_id"": ""82fbccc6bc82fea566672a8f165c0e79"", ""match"": ""verbatim"", ""chunks"": [10, 11], ""pages"": [5, 6], ""refs"": [""#/texts/48"", ""#/texts/50"", ""#/texts/51"", ""#/texts/52"", ""#/pictures/0""], ""spans"": [{""chunk"": 10, ""start"": 93, ""end"": 123}, {""chunk"": 11, ""start"": 93, ""end"": 123}]}","","","",""
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000148_32d2df447629/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000148_32d2df447629_docling_json_20260812_203724/docling/document.dclg:233:      Figure 1: Overview of SciCore-Omics. a , Model architecture of SciCore-Omics. Histological images and spatial gene expression profiles are encoded by modality-specific encoders, and projected into the language-model space. b , Unified tri-modal token representation. Image, gene virtual tokens and text tokens are jointly modelled in a shared decoder-based backbone. c , Representative downstream tasks supported by SciCore-Omics across multiple biological scales.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_01/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000246_55ef58661ddf/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000246_55ef58661ddf_docling_json_20260812_202436/docling/document.md:15:Figure 1: OmicsLM overview. Top: example questions from diverse omics datasets are formatted as multi-task conversations for OmicsLM, with each &lt;omics&gt; placeholder denoting a biological profile and gene symbols treated as explicit tokens. Bottom: each placeholder is built by mapping a single-cell or bulk expression profile, including profiles from perturbation screens, to a continuous vector and projecting it into the LLM token-embedding space, allowing multiple profiles to be interleaved in one prompt for sample-level and comparative reasoning.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003434_0c79637be8b9/full_2026_07_06_rec_003434_0c79637be8b9_docling_json_20260812_201130/debug/dense_provenance.json:933:      "text": "The End of Aging Clocks: Training Foundation Models to Reason in Aging and Longevity\nResults\nPerformance in Longevity Bench\nTo place Longevity-LLM in the context of current commercial systems, we evaluated it against 15 frontier LLMs across seven Longevity Bench tasks spanning tasks in the domains of oncology prognosis,  age  and  mortality  prediction.  Despite  having  only  14  billion  parameters,  our  SFT model ranked first in four out of seven tasks ( Figure 1 ). The base Qwen3-14B model, prior to fine-tuning, was unable to produce valid predictions on any of the tested tasks due to the inability to generate parseable outputs. The results displayed therefore have base model's effectively void metrics removed.\nThe most pronounced advantage was observed in RNA-based cancer prognosis, where the SFT model achieved 0.77 accuracy on TCGA pairwise progression-free survival comparisons (which patient lived longer without progression?). In NHANES binary 10-year mortality prediction, the SFT model placed first with 0.89 accuracy, ahead of Gemini 3 Pro which was the best model previously tested in Longevity Bench. The regression variant of the NHANES time-to-death task yielded the lowest MAE among all tested systems (51.0 months). Our SFT model also achieved 0.60 accuracy on GTEx pairwise age comparisons (which sample is older?), a task category in which most previously tested LLMs struggled to exceed random baseline.\nThese results indicate that structured fine-tuning on aging-related tasks can close the performance gap between smaller models and frontier systems of substantially greater capacity. The  improvements  are  seen  in  three  distinct  biodata  modalities,  suggesting  that  the  acquired knowledge generalizes across data types rather than being confined to a single domain.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003434_0c79637be8b9/full_2026_07_06_rec_003434_0c79637be8b9_docling_json_20260812_201130/debug/dense_provenance.json:992:      "text": "The End of Aging Clocks: Training Foundation Models to Reason in Aging and Longevity\nResults\nPerformance in Longevity Bench\nFigure 1. Longevity-LLM shows competitive performance in a range of Longevity Bench tasks. The tested tasks span the domains of transcriptomics, proteomics, and clinical record. Base Qwen-3 14B failed to produce valid outputs on all tasks.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific performance figure composed of bar charts comparing model accuracy or error across biomedical/health prediction tasks.\n\nVisible panels:\n- TCGA Survival (Binary): accuracy bar chart with chance baseline around 0.50.\n- NHANES Mortality (Binary): accuracy bar chart with chance baseline around 0.50.\n- NHANES Mortality (Pairwise): accuracy bar chart with chance baseline around 0.50.\n- NHANES Mortality (Multiclass): accuracy bar chart with chance baseline around 0.25.\n- GTEx Aging (Pairwise): accuracy bar chart with chance baseline around 0.50.\n- Proteomic Aging (Pairwise): accuracy bar chart with chance baseline around 0.50.\n- NHANES Mortality (Regression): y-axis labeled MAE, months; lower bars indicate better performance.\n\nLabels and legend:\n- Blue bars: Qwen3-14B SFT.\n- Gray bars: Frontier LLMs.\n- Red dashed line: Chance baseline.\n- Y-axes are mostly labeled Accuracy, except the regression panel labeled MAE, months.\n- X-axis labels are model names, rotated diagonally and mostly too small to read clearly.\n\nBiological or biomedical source objects:\n- TCGA survival data.\n- NHANES mortality data.\n- GTEx aging data.\n- Proteomic aging data.\nNo microscopy images, cells, tissues, molecular structures, or individual biological objects are shown.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The figure appears to summarize benchmark results from language models or frontier LLMs on biomedical prediction tasks.\n- Results are transformed into aggregate metrics: accuracy for classification/pairwise/multiclass tasks and mean absolute error in months for regression.\n\nVisible findings:\n- Qwen3-14B SFT is highlighted in blue in each panel.\n- In TCGA Survival (Binary), Qwen3-14B SFT appears to be the top-performing model.\n- In several NHANES and aging panels, Qwen3-14B SFT performs among the frontier LLM range but is not always the top bar.\n- Most models perform above the chance baseline in classification panels.\n- In NHANES Mortality Regression, Qwen3-14B SFT has one of the lower MAE values, but at least one gray model appears lower.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_06/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003434_0c79637be8b9/full_2026_07_06_rec_003434_0c79637be8b9_docling_json_20260812_201130/docling_graph/graph.html:379:        "supporting_figure_or_table": "Figure 1",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_01/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_002105_966eaaaee764/full_2026_07_06_rec_002105_966eaaaee764_docling_json_20260812_195839/docling_graph/provenance.json:4321:      "text": "Figures\nFigure 1 . Overview of the CellWhisperer multimodal AI for natural-language analysis of transcriptome data\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure describing a method called CellWhisperer for joint embedding of transcriptomes and text.\n\nPanel a: Workflow schematic titled “Joint embedding of transcriptomes and text for data exploration via natural-language chats.” It shows a dataset of 1 million paired human transcriptomes and text. Inputs include GEO metadata, expert annotations, bulk RNA-seq, single-cell RNA-seq, and gene expression counts. Sample descriptions and conversations are generated using LLMs including GPT-4 and Mixtral. The central diagram shows training and inference in a 2048-dimensional multimodal embedding space, with language and transcriptome models aligned via minimized distance and supervised training. The model interface includes text prompts, answers, transcriptome embeddings, and a generative LLM component. Applications shown on the right include free-text cell labeling, user-provided transcriptome data such as single-cell RNA-seq, and chat-style querying about cell samples.\n\nPanel b: UMAP visualization labeled “An annotated landscape of human transcriptomes.” It shows many colored clusters representing CellWhisperer embeddings of 705,430 transcriptomes from GEO. Dashed circles highlight selected regions. Biological labels connected to regions include “Activated CD8+ T Cells in Immune Response,” “Activated CD4+ T Cells in Immune Response,” “Active Myeloid Differentiation in HSPCs,” “K562 Erythroleukemia Cells in Culture,” and “Obese adipose tissue immune and metabolic state.”\n\nPanel c: UMAP visualization titled “Querying the human transcriptome landscape with natural language.” Points are colored by CellWhisperer score for the search term “leukemia,” with a blue-to-red scale. Red regions indicate higher similarity to the leukemia query.\n\nPanel d: Small histogram plots titled “GEO submission dates for the selected samples.” Three distributions are shown for selected biological query regions, with GEO submission dates spanning roughly 2013 to 2023.\n\nBiological source objects visible: human transcriptomes from GEO, bulk RNA-seq, single-cell RNA-seq, gene expression count matrices, renal carcinoma sample metadata, immune cells, HSPCs, K562 erythroleukemia cells, adipose tissue immune/metabolic samples.\n\nTransformations and interfaces: metadata and annotations are converted into sample descriptions and conversations by LLMs; transcriptomes and text are embedded into a shared multimodal space; natural-language prompts are used for cell labeling, sample search, and chat-based interpretation.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_01/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_002105_966eaaaee764/full_2026_07_06_rec_002105_966eaaaee764_docling_json_20260812_195839/docling_graph/provenance.json:5160:      "text": "Extended Data Figures\nExtended Data Figure 1 . CellWhisperer cross-modality retrieval performance and model ablation testing\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure with panels labeled **a** and **b**.\n\nPanel **a** shows cross-modality retrieval performance for a model. On the left, a bar chart titled **“Cross-modality retrieval performance of final model”** reports **Recall at top n** on a **Disease validation dataset** for two target modalities: **text** and **transcriptomes**. Bars correspond to recall at top **1, 5, 10, and 50 matches**. Reported values include approximately:\n- Text: 0.19, 0.55, 0.79, 0.97\n- Transcriptomes: 0.24, 0.59, 0.79, 0.98\n\nAlso in panel **a**, a line chart titled **“Cross-modality retrieval performance during training”** plots **Recall at top 5** versus **epoch** on the same disease validation dataset. The curve rises early and fluctuates around roughly 0.53-0.62 over training.\n\nPanel **b** is titled **“CellWhisperer ablation performance across five metrics”**. It shows a horizontal bar/dot plot with normalized scores from **0 to 1**. Rows correspond to different ablation or training settings, including examples such as:\n- LiT: both models frozen\n- Loss function: JSD\n- LiT: only language model frozen\n- LiT: both models unfrozen\n- Batch size 32, gradient accumulation 16\n- Transcriptome model: scGPT\n- Data: only GEO\n- Loss function: mean(JSD, InfoNCE)\n- Data: CELLxGENE Census\n- Augmentation: 2 annotation sets\n- Sample weights: constant\n- CellWhisperer\n\nColored points represent five evaluation metrics:\n- Tabula Sapiens zero-shot cell type classification per cluster, F1 score\n- Tabula Sapiens zero-shot batch integration, ASW label batch score\n- Tabula Sapiens zero-shot cell type integration, Avg bio score\n- Disease validation dataset retrieval, recall at 5\n- Tabula Sapiens zero-shot cell type classification per single cell, F1 score\n\nThe biological source objects referenced are **disease validation data**, **transcriptomes**, **cell types**, and **single-cell / Tabula Sapiens datasets**. The figure evaluates a cross-modal model interface between **text** and **transcriptomic representations**, including ablations of training setup, loss functions, data sources, transcriptome model choice, and augmentation/sample weighting. The visible finding is that the final model achieves high recall at larger top-k values, and the full **CellWhisperer** configuration appears among the stronger-performing settings across the normalized ablation metrics.",
